WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:02.010
- [Instructor] In this
video, we're going to talk

00:00:02.010 --> 00:00:03.333
about solubility,

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:10.100
which is just a way of describing
how well certain solutes

00:00:11.540 --> 00:00:14.613
can dissolve in certain solvents.

00:00:16.110 --> 00:00:19.420
And just as an example, we
could go to our old friend

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:22.440
sodium chloride and think about

00:00:22.440 --> 00:00:26.023
why does it dissolve well in water.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:28.740
Well, to do that, you just
have to remind yourself

00:00:28.740 --> 00:00:31.540
what water is doing when
nothing is dissolved in it.

00:00:31.540 --> 00:00:33.340
So when nothing is dissolved in it,

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:36.620
that's an oxygen attached
to two hydrogens.

00:00:36.620 --> 00:00:38.560
This end, we've talked
about in other videos,

00:00:38.560 --> 00:00:39.540
partially negative

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:41.130
'cause the electrons
like to spend more time

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:42.430
around the oxygen.

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:45.000
This is partially positive,
partially positive.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.350
And you have this hydrogen bond forming,

00:00:47.350 --> 00:00:50.560
where the partially negative
end of one water molecule

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:54.040
is attracted to the partially positive end

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:56.550
of another water molecule.

00:00:56.550 --> 00:00:58.060
That's what hydrogen bonds are all about.

00:00:58.060 --> 00:00:59.280
And we have whole videos on that,

00:00:59.280 --> 00:01:01.140
actually many videos on that.

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:06.040
The reason why sodium chloride
dissolves well in water

00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:09.400
is because sodium chloride,
as an ionic compound,

00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:12.970
it can disassociate into
its constituent ions.

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:16.770
Into a sodium cation

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:19.340
and a chloride anion.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:20.660
And we've seen this before.

00:01:20.660 --> 00:01:22.750
So that's the chloride anion,

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:27.187
this right over here is a sodium cation.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:30.670
The reason why this dissolves well

00:01:30.670 --> 00:01:33.400
is that the negative
charge is able to be drawn

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:36.760
to the positive end of
the water molecules,

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:38.090
so the hydrogen end.

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:41.410
And the positive sodium
cation is attracted

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:44.090
to the negative end of
the water molecules.

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:47.110
So what's really happening
is the attraction

00:01:47.110 --> 00:01:49.890
between the ions and the
water molecules are stronger

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:53.210
than the attraction
between the ions themselves

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:56.450
and the attraction between the
water molecules themselves.

00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:58.727
So the water molecules,
don't just bunch up and say,

00:01:58.727 --> 00:02:01.710
"We want nothing to do with
you, sodium and chloride."

00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:04.580
They say, "Hey, we're kind
of attracted to you too.

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:06.630
So why don't we mix together?"

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:09.410
We can look at things that
have less attractive forces,

00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:13.040
maybe things where the main
force is just dispersion forces.

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:16.670
If you think about a vat of pentane here,

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:18.390
they have those weak forces,

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:20.240
kind of attracting them to each other,

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:22.670
and then if you think
about a vat of hexane here,

00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:24.290
there's kind of weak forces.

00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:26.290
But if you were to put some pentane,

00:02:26.290 --> 00:02:29.170
let's call this the solute here.

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:33.540
And if you were to put it
into a solvent of hexane,

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:36.780
it will dissolve because
they are roughly as attracted

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:40.290
to each other as they are to themselves.

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:42.050
Now, what do you think is going to happen

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:45.540
if I try to put, say, some hexane,

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:47.170
if I view that as a solute,

00:02:47.170 --> 00:02:49.230
and I were to put it in water?

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:51.080
Well, in that situation,

00:02:51.080 --> 00:02:54.070
the water is going to be
far more attracted to itself

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:57.090
than it's going to be
attracted to the hexane.

00:02:57.090 --> 00:02:59.840
Let's say that this is the water here.

00:02:59.840 --> 00:03:03.010
You're going to have these
globs of the hydrocarbon form

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:05.260
because the water is
more attracted to itself.

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:08.230
It's not easy for the
hydrocarbon to dissolve.

00:03:08.230 --> 00:03:11.100
Now, there are many organic
molecules that do dissolve well.

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:13.470
And that's usually because
they have some part

00:03:13.470 --> 00:03:16.830
of the molecule that
has some polarity to it.

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:21.740
One example is ethanol,
which has an OH group.

00:03:21.740 --> 00:03:24.960
But ethanol, which has
a chain of two carbons,

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:27.280
when we talk about alcohol
in everyday language,

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:29.760
drinking alcohol, that is ethanol.

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:33.090
There's many other anols,
many other alcohols,

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:35.146
but this is ethanol here.

00:03:35.146 --> 00:03:36.330
And if you were to take
alcohol and you were to mix it

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:38.570
in water, it does dissolve well.

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:40.870
And that's because this oxygen here,

00:03:40.870 --> 00:03:42.370
it's more electronegative

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:44.320
than the things that it is bonded to.

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:47.260
And so it still hogs
electrons a little bit.

00:03:47.260 --> 00:03:49.440
And so you still have that
partially negative charge.

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:53.420
You still have that
polarity to the molecule.

00:03:53.420 --> 00:03:55.260
And so that's able to attract it

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:58.810
to neighboring water molecules,
which allows it to dissolve.

00:03:58.810 --> 00:04:00.650
But you could imagine
if you had an alcohol

00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:03.820
that had a much, much longer carbon chain,

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:06.940
so you had 10 carbons or 15 carbons,

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:09.288
then all of a sudden
the relative proportion

00:04:09.288 --> 00:04:10.260
of how polar it is compared to

00:04:10.260 --> 00:04:12.070
how large of the molecule it is,

00:04:12.070 --> 00:04:15.320
it'll make it harder and
harder for it to dissolve

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:18.260
in a polar solvent like water.

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:22.363
And so our mega takeaway here
is that like dissolves like.

